WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.304
Three more ways a public number misleads.

2
00:00:02.584 --> 00:00:04.376
Implementation sensitivity.

3
00:00:04.656 --> 00:00:08.347
The same benchmark run through different code gives different numbers.

4
00:00:08.627 --> 00:00:22.813
Hugging Face’s 2023 analysis of its Open LLM Leaderboard found that three implementations of MMLU gave substantially different scores for the same models and even reversed the order of two of them.

5
00:00:23.093 --> 00:00:27.659
Prompt format, answer extraction, and the number of examples shown all matter.

6
00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:30.648
Numbers from different harnesses aren’t comparable.

7
00:00:30.928 --> 00:00:32.251
Selective reporting.

8
00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:45.416
“The Leaderboard Illusion” (Singh and colleagues, 2025) reported that some providers privately tested many variants on the Arena leaderboard before release and could choose which scores to publish.

9
00:00:45.696 --> 00:00:49.771
Launch posts naturally feature the benchmarks a model does well on.

10
00:00:50.051 --> 00:00:55.469
Arena’s own style-control analysis found answer length and formatting sway human votes.

11
00:00:55.749 --> 00:00:59.568
None of this makes the numbers false; it makes them a selected sample.

12
00:00:59.848 --> 00:01:01.128
Vendor-run results.

13
00:01:01.408 --> 00:01:11.115
Most headline numbers are run by the vendor, with the vendor’s scaffold, prompts, settings, and sometimes several attempts per task or a custom agent harness.

14
00:01:11.395 --> 00:01:21.464
Check the footnotes for the number of attempts, the effort setting, the tools allowed, and whether the comparison numbers came from the competitors’ own reports or a common harness.

15
00:01:21.744 --> 00:01:27.312
Independent replications and leaderboards that run every model through one common harness help.

16
00:01:27.592 --> 00:01:39.581
Even a carefully curated benchmark, such as SWE-bench Verified (a human-filtered subset of SWE-bench tasks made with OpenAI), measures its task, not yours.

17
00:01:39.861 --> 00:01:46.432
And one statistical check on all of them: if a leaderboard shows no intervals, apply lesson 7 yourself.

18
00:01:46.712 --> 00:01:52.984
Two models 1 point apart on a 500-question benchmark are almost certainly not distinguishable.
